post_install() {
    systemd-sysusers noti-mapper.conf >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MESSAGE'

noti-mapper is installed but not configured. Nothing is watched yet.

  1. Copy the examples from /usr/share/doc/noti-mapper/examples/ into
     /etc/noti-mapper.d/ and edit them. They are not live where they are.

  2. If you use secrets, put them in /etc/noti-mapper/secrets.json. The daemon
     reads it as the noti-mapper user and refuses to start if anyone else can
     read it, so:

       install -m 0640 -o root -g noti-mapper secrets.json /etc/noti-mapper/

     Root owns it and the daemon only reads it, so a compromised daemon cannot
     rewrite its own credentials. 0600 owned by noti-mapper also works.

  3. Check your work:      noti-mapper validate
  4. Then:                 systemctl enable --now noti-mapper

The IMAP reader in the example ships with "dry_run": true. Leave it that way
for a week and read the journal. It will tell you what it would have fired on,
which is how you discover that your carrier also sends "Out for delivery"
twice a day.

The HomeKit output adds one accessory to your existing Apple Home. It does not
migrate, bridge, or re-pair anything you already own. It does need mDNS on the
same L2 segment as your Home hub; if your IoT devices are on their own VLAN you
need an mDNS reflector.

MESSAGE
}

post_upgrade() {
    systemd-sysusers noti-mapper.conf >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

pre_remove() {
    systemctl --quiet disable --now noti-mapper.service 2>/dev/null || true
}

post_remove() {
    cat <<'MESSAGE'

/var/lib/noti-mapper still holds latch state, and /etc/noti-mapper.d still
holds your configuration. Neither is removed, in case this is a reinstall.

MESSAGE
}
